---
layout: changelog
title: Announcing Appwrite 2.7.1 for self-hosted deployments
date: 2026-11-04
cover: /images/changelog/2026-11-04.avif
tags: security, functions, sites, infrastructure
---

Appwrite 2.7.1 is now available for self-hosting. This is a security release for the 2.7 line. We strongly recommend upgrading your instance to apply the fix.

This release includes:

- The Open Runtimes executor updated from `1.1.2` to `1.3.1`. The old executor followed symlinks that a function or site planted in its logs, build output or build cache, so a deployment could read files from the executor container, including `_APP_EXECUTOR_SECRET`. With that secret, code in a function can call the executor directly, and the executor controls Docker on the host.
- Executor support for rotating its secret without downtime through `OPR_EXECUTOR_SECRET_PREVIOUS`, a storage DSN check that is off by default, and an opt-in streamed response format. None of these change existing behavior.

You are affected if anyone you don't fully trust can deploy functions or sites on your instance. Upgrade, then rotate `_APP_EXECUTOR_SECRET` by setting a new value in `.env` and running `docker compose up -d`.

2.7.1 keeps the same 1.2.138 Console as 2.7.0 and doesn't change the database schema, so upgrading from 2.7.0 only replaces the images. If you maintain your own `docker-compose.yml`, set the `openruntimes-executor` image to `openruntimes/executor:1.3.1`.

